  "question": {
    "text": "Let $\\vec{\\alpha}=\\hat{i}+\\hat{j}+\\hat{k}, \\vec{\\beta}=\\hat{i}-\\hat{j}-\\hat{k}$ and\r\n$\\vec{\\gamma}=-\\hat{i}+\\hat{j}-\\hat{k}$ be three vectors. A vector $\\vec{\\delta},$ in the plane of $\\vec{\\alpha}$ and $\\vec{\\beta}$, whose projection on $\\vec{\\gamma}$ is $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},$ is given by"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "$-\\hat{i}-3 \\hat{j}-3 \\hat{k}$",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "$\\hat{i}-3 \\hat{j}-3 \\hat{k}$",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "$-\\hat{i}+3 \\hat{j}+3 \\hat{k}$",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "$\\hat{i}+3 \\hat{j}-3 \\hat{k}$",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "C"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "We have,\r\n$\\overrightarrow{8}=\\vec{a}+\\lambda \\vec{\\beta}$\r\n$=(\\hat{i}+\\hat{j}+\\hat{k})+\\lambda(\\hat{i}-\\hat{j}-\\hat{k})$\r\n$\\Rightarrow \\quad \\vec{\\delta}=(1+\\lambda) \\hat{i}+(1-\\lambda) \\hat{j}+(1-\\lambda) \\hat{k}$\r\nGiven, $\\quad \\frac{\\vec{\\delta} \\cdot \\vec{\\gamma}}{|\\vec{\\gamma}|}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$\r\n$\\Rightarrow \\frac{(1+\\lambda)(-1)+(1-\\lambda)(1)+(1-\\lambda)(-1)}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$\r\n$\\Rightarrow \\quad \\lambda=-2$\r\n$\\therefore$\r\n$\\vec{\\delta}=-\\hat{i}+3 \\hat{j}+3 \\hat{k}$"
  }
}